# Why is this change being made?

This change enables field level authorization when querying objects in GraphQL.

- Implied anonymous is pending in #509
- Field Authorization for mutations will immediately follow this PR.


# What does this change do?

The internals of this change can be read in the [design doc](https://microsoft.sharepoint.com/:w:/t/AzureDataMultiverseExperience/ETf-KN5O9PhFgF-a2HmuGZoBDrHasQFUF_p2AVWQMA9UaQ?e=Q6MBAo) under the section Field Authorization and are copied here for convenience.
The `@authorize` directive is added to all the relevant fields on the [ObjectTypeDefinition](https://graphql.org/learn/schema/#object-types-and-fields).

## Background

Field Authorization defines how fields can be restricted to certain authorization role requirements defined by the runtime configuration.

## Implementation

The Hawaii GraphQL Schema Builder generates fields for a schema’s Query type. The `Query` type is a 

> special object type that defines all of the top-level entry points for queries that clients execute against your server. ([Apollo Docs](https://www.apollographql.com/docs/apollo-server/schema/schema/#the-query-type))

Each of the generated fields represents a query, more specifically a read operation on a particular object type. For each object type (entity) defined in the runtime configuration, two queries are generated:

1.	`publishers`(plural form of the entity name, in this example: Publisher) for reading many records
2.	`publisher_by_pk` for reading one record of the object type, in this example: Publisher

The generated queries take the following form in the GraphQL schema:
```graphql
type Query {
  publishers(
    """
    Arguments (first, after, _filter, and orderBy) are omitted for brevity. 
HotChocolate only supports adding the @authorize directive fields and types.([Hot Chocolate Docs](https://chillicream.com/docs/hotchocolate/security/authorization#usage)),
so argument authorization is not in scope of field authorization at this time.
    """
  ): PublisherConnection!

  publisher_by_pk(id: Int!): Publisher
}
```

Each query defines the return type of the fetch operation. For fetching many records, the return type is the pagination compatible object `PublisherConnection`, whose type definition defines the field items with a return type of `Publisher`.
``` graphql
"""
The return object from a filter query that supports a pagination token for paging through results
"""
type PublisherConnection {
  """
  The list of items that matched the filter
  """
  items: [Publisher!]!

  """
  A pagination token to provide to subsequent pages of a query
  """
  endCursor: String

  """
  Indicates if there are more pages of items to return
  """
  hasNextPage: Boolean!
}
```

Because all query operations ultimately return the object type, the object type will be the location where the `@authorize` directive should be placed field-level. The GraphQL schema object type definition will look like the following:
``` graphql
type Publisher {
  id: Int! @authorize(roles:[“role1”, “role2”, “roleN”])
  name: String! @authorize(roles:[“role1”, “role2”, “roleN”])

  books(
    """
    Arguments (first, after, _filter, and orderBy) are omitted for brevity.
    """
  ): BookConnection!
}
```

The above example denotes the field _books_, which resolves the book type, where the `@authorize` directive would be present on its fields.

Because the object type definition contains the `@authorize` directive, read operations on this object will be required to respect read permissions defined on the entity. This will be applicable on mutations since mutations define not only input types, but also define a return type that fetches the created, updated, or deleted object on which the mutation was performed.

