# Examples

### Runtime Configuration
``` json
"entities": {
    "Publisher": {
      "source": "publishers",
      "rest": true,
      "graphql": true,
      "permissions": [
        {
          "role": "anonymous",
          "actions": [ "create",
            {
              "action": "read",
              "fields": {
                "include": [ "*" ],
                "exclude": [ "name" ]
              }
            },
              "update",
              "delete" ]
        },
        {
          "role": "authenticated",
          "actions": [ "create", "read", "update", "delete" ]
        }
]}}
```

### GraphQL Query
```gql
{
    publisher_by_pk(id: 1234) {
        id
    }
}
```

### GraphQL Response
```json
{
    "data": {
        "publisher_by_pk": {
            "id": 1234
        }
    }
}
```

However, the following would result in a GraphQL error (unauthorized), per [HotChocolate Docs:](https://chillicream.com/docs/hotchocolate/security/authorization#usage) this still is a HTTP 200 response
by design, while the error messaging is in the response body.

### GraphQL Query
```gql
{
    publisher_by_pk(id: 1234) {
        id,
name
    }
}
```

### GraphQL Response
```json
{
    "errors": [
        {
            "message": "The current user is not authorized to access this resource.",
            "locations": [
                {
                    "line": 4,
                    "column": 9
                }
            ],
            "path": [
                "publisher_by_pk",
                "name"
            ],
            "extensions": {
                "code": "AUTH_NOT_AUTHORIZED"
            }
        }
    ],
    "data": {
        "publisher_by_pk": null
    }
}
```

